+\section{Simulation Design in Plain Radar Terms}
+This section states the benchmark in the order a reviewer expects: target, environment, sensor products, detector views, and scoring. The positive class is an open-source inspired fixed-wing pusher-prop public proxy. It uses public geometry and kinematic priors, broad RCS/aspect envelopes, and pusher-prop cadence priors, but it does not import any measured Iranian-drone radar signature. The scenario phases have physical meaning: initial take-up stresses ground coupling and unsettled kinematics; climb transition stresses changing aspect, range rate, and cue consistency; cruise altitude stresses the steadier low-altitude track case.
+
+Monte Carlo sampling is used to create many independent scenario groups rather than many nearly identical rows. A scenario group fixes the positive/negative role, site archetype, range band, aspect, noise regime, hard-negative family, and split assignment before phase expansion. The group then expands into the three phase windows. This is the core leakage defense: a target-like sequence cannot appear in both train/CV and holdout merely because different phases were split row-wise.
+
+The generated radar product is not intended to be a fielded sensor replica. It is a controlled signal-processing workbench. The radar branch cards define the assumed carrier, bandwidth, CPI, PRF/CRF, pulse count, range bins, range resolution, Doppler resolution, and velocity-equivalent Doppler spacing. Target energy is modulated by range, aspect, RCS fluctuation, Doppler centroid, and proxy micro-Doppler terms. The environment injects SNR/CNR buckets, Weibull/K-like clutter, sea/weather/terrain glint stressors, vegetation motion, multipath ghosts, RFI bursts, AGC compression, clock drift, quantization, dropped CPIs, Doppler folding, and calibration offsets. These are standard radar-stress categories for detector comparison, not site-calibrated truth.
+
