+\section{Detector Processing Baselines and Sensor Fusion}
+After synthetic sensing, the paper compares three increasingly strong classes of processing. First, classical radar and cue branches summarize range-Doppler concentration, micro-Doppler spread, CFAR/MTD-style responses, track confidence, acoustic cadence, passive-RF no-signal/RFI/provenance features, and fusion-context features. These are not vendor processors; they are role-compatible public envelopes grounded in standard radar detection, CFAR, micro-Doppler, and classifier-calibration practice \cite{rohling1983,chen2011,kay1998,fawcett2006,platt1999,niculescu2005}.
+
+Second, tabular and sequence ML baselines test whether ordinary learned processing can extract signal from the allowed detector views. These lanes are useful controls, but they are not the accepted-practice comparator.
+
+Third, accepted prior fusion combines radar/cue branch scores through a human-engineered late-fusion policy with train/CV thresholding. This is the main non-EI comparator because low-altitude UAS detection is naturally multimodal: radar evidence, acoustic cadence, passive-RF missingness/provenance, and stale-track/false-track context are each incomplete alone. Fusion is powerful because it can trade off complementary failure modes, but it must remain auditable and calibrated.
+
