 \section{Raw Samples and Compare-Only Anchor}
 The raw sample figures are visual diagnostics, not detector features. Figure~\ref{fig:radarcard} exposes the model card and signal-chain assumptions, while Figure~\ref{fig:rdpanel} shows range-Doppler proxy views for a positive public-proxy group and a hard-negative group across the three phase windows.
 
+\section{Engineered Intelligence as Auditable Algorithm Innovation}
+Engineered Intelligence is not presented as magic and not as a black box. It is an auditable workflow for autonomous algorithm innovation: decompose the detection problem into score families, evolve candidate combinations on train/CV rows, optimize sparse nonnegative weights against explicit objectives, calibrate the selected score with a monotone odds map, and freeze the result before holdout scoring.
+
+The important claim is methodological as well as numerical. Conventional static algorithms encode one human design at a time. EI instead creates a reviewable search loop over candidate algorithms and can be retargeted to richer objectives: lower false-alarm burden for specific hard-negative families, stronger calibration, lower Brier score, better phase balance, smaller component count, or robustness under specific clutter/noise regimes. In this paper we lock one objective family around low-FPR operating behavior because it is the clearest rare-positive radar decision metric.
+
+When available, the evidence lane records train/CV search traces and selected-lock metadata. Figure~\ref{fig:eiprogress} should be read as an internal-discovery diagnostic, not an extra holdout claim. The holdout result remains the frozen Table~\ref{tab:metrics} score.
